  "summary": "Three preprints tie circuit architecture to locomotor control and to inference of connectivity from simulated spiking activity.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that circuit dynamics depend on architecture and operating regime. The antiphase synchronization preprint introduces a composite metric for identifying antiphase locking in a biomorphic spiking central pattern generator embedded in a neuromuscular loop. The Rat v2 preprint pairs a biomechanical rat body with Rat NCAP, a spinal-circuit-based neural architecture, and GPU training infrastructure for simulating locomotion. The third preprint reverses the direction of inference, showing in silico how spatial constraints and dynamical regime affect recovery of structural connectivity from simulated spiking network activity. What is new is the explicit link between architectural priors and locomotor control, and between regime dependence and structural inference. The items do not share a common method or system, so the connection rests on the architecture-dynamics relationship rather than on shared tooling.",
